\begin{abstract}
This thesis presents the full mathematical, computational, and physical 
foundations of the Sovereign Astrophysical Simulation Architecture (SASA-v4.0), 
a high-performance numerical engine designed to map multi-messenger 
astrophysical telemetry into terrestrial lithospheric stress fields. The 
framework evolves hyper-state vectors over the 11-dimensional warped product 
manifold $\mathcal{N}_{11} = \mathcal{M}_4 \times \mathcal{X}_6 \times S^1$, 
utilizing a non-local entanglement kernel, an oracle-extended Hilbert space, 
and a Cayley-stable Hamiltonian evolution operator. Real-time cryptographic 
ingestion, spectral stability analysis, and seismic neutralization algorithms 
are validated through full-scale laboratory stress tests.
\end{abstract}

